==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|embelishen}}, from {{der|en|fro|embellir}}, from {{m|fro|em-}} + {{m|fro|bel}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪmˈbɛlɪʃ/|/ɛm-/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-embellish.ogg|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To make more [[beautiful]] and [[attractive]] by adding [[ornamentation]]; to [[decorate]].
#: {{ux|en|The old book cover was '''embellished''' with golden letters}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1998
|author=C.H. Knowles
|chapter=Henry III (1207-1272; r. 1216-72)
|title=Medieval England: An Encyclopedia
|url=https://archive.org/details/medievalenglande0000unse_z8d0/
|publisher=w:Garland Publishing
|ISBN=0-8240-5786-4
|LCCN=97-35523
|OCLC=37608196
|page=345
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/medievalenglande0000unse_z8d0/page/345/
|column=2
|text=He was a notable patron of the arts who lavishly '''embellished''' his royal residences and rebuilt and enlarged Westminster Abbey, destined as the burialplace of himself and many of his successors, in honor of his patron saint, Edward the Confessor.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} To [[enhance]] by adding something not [[strictly]] [[integral]] or [[necessary]].
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=ii|page=25|passage=A Scythian Shepherd, so '''imbelliſhed'''<br>With Natures pride, and richeſt furniture?<br>His looks do menace heauen & dare the Gods,<br>His fiery eies are fixt vpon the earth.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Doyle|title=Arsenal's Theo Walcott hits hat-trick in thrilling victory over Newcastle|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/football/2012/dec/29/arsenal-newcastle-united-premier-league|newspaper=w:The Guardian|location=London|date=29 December 2012|passage={{w|Lukas Podolski|Podolski}} gave {{w|Theo Walcott|Walcott}} a chance to further '''embellish''' Arsenal's first-half performance when he eluded {{w|James Perch}} and slipped the ball through to the striker.}}
# To make something sound or look [[better]] or more [[acceptable]] than it is in reality; to [[distort]], to [[embroider]].
#: {{coi|en|to '''embellish''' a story, the truth}}

